  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nEarly This Morning: Lingering Rain, Fog  \n\nRain continues for portions of Northeast Iowa and far Southwest \nWisconsin early this morning as the frontal boundary sits just to \nour west near the Twin Cities metro. As the frontal boundary slowly \npushes to the east/southeast, so will the rain. Most impacts across \nthe area will be in the way for low clouds and fog thanks to the \nresidual low level/surface moisture from recent rainfall. Fog, dense \nin some areas, will linger through morning, burning off a few hours \nafter sunrise. This will likely impact the morning commute for some \nso be sure to leave yourself a few extra minutes on the roads. \n\nToday - Thursday: Drier, Near-Normal Temperatures \n\nWinds from the west finally start to pick up through the day today, \nwhich will help to usher in a slightly drier airmass, even though \nthe frontal boundary still won't be fully through the area. A \nsurface high pressure building in from the Plains will help to push \nthe boundary fully through the area by day break Thursday and should \nkeep our forecast dry for the mid/late week time period. With weak \nupper level ridging set to build in, even though a \"cold\" front \nmoved through, temperatures will remain near normal in the upper 70s \nto low 80s. \n\nFriday - This Weekend: Rain Chances Return, Slight Cool Down \n\nThings look to get a bit more active as we head into the weekend \nwith a pronounced upper trough sweeping across Canada/The Northern \nCONUS. While the base of the upper low will be well to our north, \nlikely across the Hudson Bay, the trough/trough axis will extend \nwell south into the CONUS and so will the cold front from the \nsubsequent surface system. Return flow is expected to be strong \nFriday afternoon, allowing for ample destabilization across the \nNorthern Plains and Upper Midwest. Combined with the potential for \ndecent shear from the passage of the upper level shortwave and \nsynoptic lift from the cold front, both AI NWP forecasts and the SPC \nDay 4 Convective Outlook are highlighting much of Minnesota \n(including portions of Southeast Minnesota) for severe weather \npotential Friday night into early Saturday morning. Currently, the \nsevere potential for our area is still questionable as the timing of \nthe trough passage will play a critical role in whether convection \nwould make it to our area before instability starts to wane. Models \nkeep going back and forth on this timing so this will be something \nto pay attention to over the coming days. Overall the better \npotential seems to favor Central/South Central Minnesota and areas \nfurther west. Otherwise, expect slightly cooler temperatures behind \nthis cold front with highs in the 70s for the weekend. Yet another \nchance for rain returns Monday with both and EC/GEFS showing a stout \nupper trough ejecting out of the Pacific Northwest and towards the \nPlains. LREF Grand Ensemble (GEFS/EC/GEPS) probabilities for \nprecipitation on Monday are already between 65-85% 6 days out. This \nwill be something to watch from a heavy rain perspective.",
